     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I understand you're taking responsibility for it. But 5,100 mg/day of metformin is about double the maximum daily dose, and it has no therapeutic benefit over 2,550 mg/day. It does substantially raise the risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function. I can't think of a clinical scenario where it's appropriate, so I'm not comfortable placing it.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter the maximum standard dose:** metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day).\n- **Enter a different dose:** any dose within labeled limits, for example 1,000 mg twice daily or 500 mg three times daily.\n- **Add a note to the order:** for example, \"Titrate per glycemic response; pharmacy to review.\"\n\nIf you believe a dose above the labeled maximum is warranted, I'd suggest going through pharmacy or your institution's formal override process. They can verify it independently, and I can't do that.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
